# web/ is served from disk byte-for-byte, and the LAN and VPS builds are
# compared byte-for-byte against web/index.html by scripts/build_mobile_web.py.
# A checkout must therefore hand back exactly the bytes that were committed.
#
# Git for Windows installs with core.autocrlf=true at the system level, which
# rewrites every LF in the repository as CRLF on disk. This repository carried
# no .gitattributes, so a fresh clone silently received CRLF copies of files the
# repository stores as LF, and every guard that compares bytes or checksums a
# shipped asset failed on a clone that had changed nothing.
web/**/*.html text eol=lf
web/**/*.js text eol=lf
web/**/*.json text eol=lf
web/**/*.md text eol=lf

# apps/static/jsqr.min.js is the same scanner web/static/jsqr.min.js ships, the
# two are compared byte-for-byte, and the bundled copy is checksummed against a
# fixed digest. Pinning web/ alone made the two copies disagree instead of
# agreeing on the wrong bytes.
apps/static/**/*.js text eol=lf
apps/static/**/*.json text eol=lf
apps/static/**/*.md text eol=lf

# Images are binary and must never be converted. This is not theoretical: the
# rules above were first written as "web/** text eol=lf", which also marked the
# six PNGs as text, and Git then rewrote the single CRLF byte pair that occurs
# in each one's compressed data. Every fresh clone reported those six files as
# permanently modified, so build_release - which refuses to build from a dirty
# tree - could not produce a certified release from a clone at all, and a commit
# made inside such a clone would have baked the rewritten bytes in for good.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.bmp binary
*.webp binary
